<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Applied ML · Ranking · Leakage Control</div><h1>Term Deposit Customer Ranking</h1><p class="section-lead">Can a bank get more term-deposit subscriptions from the same calling capacity by ranking already eligible customers before the call?</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<h2>Business decision</h2>
<p>The output is not primarily a yes/no classifier. It is a pre-call ranking score used to order eligible customers so limited calling capacity is spent on the most promising records first.</p>

<h2>Data & decision timing</h2>
<ul>
<li>40,000 historical calls.</li>
<li>2,896 subscriptions: a 7.24% conversion rate.</li>
<li>13 original predictor fields; 12 are valid before the call.</li>
<li><code>duration</code> is only known after the call finishes, so it is retained solely as a leakage diagnostic and excluded from every deployable model.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Models & validation</h2>
<p>Logistic Regression provides the transparent linear candidate and HistGradientBoosting the nonlinear challenger. Model selection uses PR-AUC as the primary objective, but deployment judgement also considers ordered robustness, lift, interpretability and monitoring simplicity.</p>
<ul>
<li>Untouched 20% holdout: tuned HGB PR-AUC / ROC-AUC = <strong>0.272820 / 0.733930</strong>; tuned LR = <strong>0.240265 / 0.709681</strong>.</li>
<li>Expanding-window ordered validation: LR = <strong>0.112626 / 0.566290</strong>; HGB = <strong>0.096333 / 0.551600</strong>.</li>
<li>The large shuffled-to-ordered performance drop is treated as a central robustness warning, not hidden.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">Prediction timing is part of the model definition. The post-call <code>duration</code> feature produces a large retrospective performance jump, which is exactly why it is evidence of leakage rather than a deployable predictor.</div>

<h2>Business-facing result</h2>
<p>Across eight eligible forward blocks, Logistic Regression captured <strong>228 subscribers in the top-ranked 5%</strong> of calls versus about <strong>123 expected under random calling</strong> at the same volume: <strong>1.853857× lift</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>5% call depth: <strong>1.853857×</strong> lift.</li>
<li>10% call depth: <strong>1.606389×</strong> lift.</li>
<li>20% call depth: <strong>1.401474×</strong> lift.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<p>Use <strong>unweighted Logistic Regression with all 12 valid pre-call features</strong> as the primary candidate in a controlled prospective pilot, with HGB retained as a challenger. Treat raw scores as ranking signals rather than literal subscription probabilities until prospective recalibration is available.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can identify leakage, align modelling choices with the actual decision point, compare transparent and nonlinear models under conventional and ordered validation, and translate model scores into a capacity-aware operating recommendation.</p>

